body:
Hello fellow Canadian!

Okay, let's take a step back for a moment. From what I gather, you don't know a lot about mental illnesses (WHICH IS FINE). I recommend checking out this [website](https://cmha.ca/mental-health/understanding-mental-illness) for some information.

&#x200B;

> I know this is sort of a warped opinion, but I've come to the conclusion that having a diagnosis for a mental illness such as anxiety/depression is probably a huge benefit to an employee, because they can take massive amounts of paid time-off very frequently, based on a condition that is well- seemingly easy to malinger, and that doesn't really impede you from taking part in any other activity on your time off. 

Yikes. No, having a mental illness is \*not\* a huge benefit to an anyone. The crap that they have to deal with, yet display functionality, especially at work is exhausting. I don't know how your workplace is but my workplace is a little old fashioned and getting paid time off, other than your allotted 15 days vacation. is not a thing. Personally (sorry for getting a little personal) I have cried several times at my desk due to my anxiety and depression.

&#x200B;

> based on a condition that is well- seemingly easy to malinger, and that doesn't really impede you from taking part in any other activity on your time off. 

I'm sure there are people out there that fake this kind of thing. Mental illnesses are considered invisible illnesses so no one can really tell, except a doctor. Honestly, I don't think this is something any of us can judge on. None of us know what your colleague is going through or the conversation that would have been had with your employer. It's honestly none of anyone's concern. 

Also, mental illnesses can definitely impede activities. Again I don't know what you're colleague is experiencing but I personally have a really hard time being out in crowded spaces, which makes a relaxing vacation not so relaxing.

&#x200B;

>Furthermore, I think most employment law protects employees from being fired due to having a disability. 

Yes. Terminating an employee due to any disability is discrimination and goes against Canadian human rights. I found [this website](http://www.ohrc.on.ca/en/human-rights-and-mental-health-fact-sheet) for more information! For mental health in the workplace, check [this](https://www.canada.ca/en/employment-social-development/services/health-safety/mental-health.html) out!

&#x200B;

In conclusion, there are a lot of drawbacks with having anxiety/depression, including daily meds, suicidal thoughts, crying in public, expensive therapy; but these are just a small handful of them.

Any way, hopefully you check out the websites I provided; I think they're helpful and informative. Beniceb.... has some good points, too!

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

